00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.760
So my name is Zachary Rogers

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.260
I live in Auburn, California

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.160
Which is about 40 miles North East of Sacramento

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.300
I go to *unintelligible* high school

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.300
And I'm the director of photography for our advanced

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.360
TV production class

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.700
When I was a kid I was mystified by movies

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.540
And you know where you're going?

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.020
You're planning on going to film school...?

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.540
Or are you still searching?

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.540
I've kinda pulled it together in the past few weeks

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.940
Of what exactly, like, my plan

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:37.980
Plan for graduation is

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.000
And what I've come up with so far is I'm gonna go to

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.040
LACC, Los Angeles City College

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.400
And they have a film course there

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.020
It's kinda like a poor mans UCLA

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.320
And get a certificate of cinema and TV production

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:49.960
So I'll just be taking

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.560
Movie making classes 20 hours - 25 hours a week

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.180
Then the time I'm not doing that I can be working on set

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:58.520
Trying to get internships or just working a 9-5 job

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.120
How many videos would you say you've completed up to this point?

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:04.220
Around ten. Maybe fifteen.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:05.820
It seems like... bad

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:06.720
Right, you don't know what you're doing

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:07.520
Yeah, exactly

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:08.560
That's how you find out.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:09.060
Yeah

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.260
So is that the first lesson?

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:11.240
It is.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.320
Just pick up a camera and start shooting?

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:13.920
Yup

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.120
Is that you too

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.300
Can be a digi-maker

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.280
What part of working in this field do you find most satisfying?

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.440
I think when you have the opportunity to

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.900
Get feedback from people that are watching your stuff

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:37.940
And they're giving you

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:39.600
Unbiased feedback

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.600
One of my favorite things is

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.360
After we put out a video to go through the comments

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.000
And see what people say

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:47.500
Yeah

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.160
Do they like it? Do they hate it?

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:49.960
Do they...

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.040
What do they like about it?

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.400
Yeah, what do they like about it?

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:54.800
And have that

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:56.620
Engagement

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.000
And that's what actually makes YouTube really cool

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.040
Is because it allows you to do that immediately *snap*

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.220
So I really searched through the YouTube comments too to see

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:06.900
If anyone had questions that weren't being answered

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.520
Or just kinda overlook

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.500
One major thing that I saw a lot is

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.240
How exactly do you guys make money?

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:14.900
Because you do -

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:15.860
I ask myself that everyday.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.420
You do upload, y'know, very high quality videos

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.620
That on average break a million view mark

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.340
So how do you supply all of this

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.520
With all the salaries and all the, y'know

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:28.100
Yeah

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:28.900
Cos' I mean, you're a family man

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:32.600
How do you, exactly support all that with an actual income and salary

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.460
Totally, it's a good question

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.000
The first

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.420
And, I guess, most basic answer

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.260
Is that it's multiple different income streams

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:46.080
That are all based around the product

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.440
Of what we make here, which is video

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:50.300
In the beginning of every video there's an ad

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.520
So we make money, first and foremost, through that

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:57.420
That, in and of itself, is kind of it's own science

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.480
With YouTube, the basic rule is that minutes watched

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:06.040
Are your key factor to how much you get paid from it

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:11.220
But realistically we only get paid about 80 cents for every thousand people that watch the video

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.100
So the next part of that is the Sam and Niko videos

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:16.880
Which usually are a little bit longer

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:19.140
Something that's a little bit easier to shoot

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:22.780
And, generally, we get paid a little bit more from those

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:24.180
Than we do from the Corridor videos

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.260
What part of working in this field do you find most challenging?

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:29.860
Oh, that's a good question

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:31.280
Pace of...

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:35.360
...Creating videos

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:38.120
It's...

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.040
A challenge not in the sense that it's always hard

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:45.940
But in that there's always a new thing to be learned

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:49.560
And understanding that each time you go through the creative process

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.380
There's going to be something that you're gonna need to learn

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:58.940
And accepting that and then, like, building it into how you get to the end goal

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:00.620
Is really important

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:02.500
And it's challenging

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.820
But it's a good kind of challenge, y'know, it's a healthy kind of challenge

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:09.340
So that can be as simple as like trying to figure out a camera movement in this one shot

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:09.980
Sure

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:15.000
Or figuring out how to edit with footage that you don't have enough to tell

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.460
The original story

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:21.260
And having to go through the process of changing the edit to tell a different story

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:22.860
Than you originally sought out to

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:24.840
And then learning... that

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.040
And then how you apply it to what you do in the future

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:28.860
I mean, it comes in many different formats

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.380
But it's always there

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:33.600
And in an arts - in an artistic career

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.040
That will always be a truth

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:37.940
Attention to detail

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:41.160
Whatever you're good at you understand the technical side of how it works

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.240
And that's part of what makes you good at it

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:44.640
What is your routine?

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:45.560
Dedication

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:47.420
And consistency

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.560
How you work with other people

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.960
Going more into that creative partner sort of thing

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:55.660
Like once you understand the technical side and you're dedicated and you have a routine

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:58.360
Then the next thing, really, is to look for

00:07:58.360 --> 00:07:59.720
Creative partners that you can

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:01.240
That you can work with

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:04.380
Because even though there may not be the people in your...

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.960
Area, or in your everday situation

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.700
Eventually if you continue to reach out and

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:15.000
Don't be shy about it you will find people that

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:17.040
That work well with you

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.460
You never wanna live in a creative vacuum

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.240
It's always important to have your own creative identity

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:25.100
And to understand creatively what YOU want

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:26.540
And listen to your creative voice

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:27.760
And follow that

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.480
But it's always great to have people that you can

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.060
Bounce ideas off of, people that you can creatively collaborate with

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:36.520
And people that bring other skill sets to the table

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:38.440
That you might not necessarily have

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:42.380
When you're younger and you want to make art, it's all about

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:44.160
**** the man

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:48.200
That's where the core of a lot of creative inspiration comes from

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:50.980
The way that all art is supported

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:53.580
Is by commercial

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.020
So it's very...

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:00.260
It's a very interesting thing about Western Society

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:03.720
Back in the Greek and Roman days

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:06.580
Artists would be commissioned by

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.920
the Emperors, or the kings, or the Government

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:11.340
To create art

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:15.280
But in the United States at least we don't have a system for that

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.240
So all art is supported by commercial enterprise

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:20.760
It's a little bit of a Catch 22

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:24.660
In the sense that you wanna create something that's unique and you wanna own it and

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:28.200
You want it to be original but at the same time it has to be relate able

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:30.320
And it has to be sell able

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:34.060
And you always wanna find out where that is, and it's totally okay to sometimes be like

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:37.240
"You know what? I just wanna make this and get it out there"

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:39.420
"It's a creative thing I have inside of me"

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.260
"I don't care if anyone likes it"

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:44.580
And you just wanna make it and get it out there -- That's totally awesome and fine

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.880
Film school vs. Just kinda going out and learning how to do it

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:51.100
Some people choose to go to film school, some people choose to

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:53.620
Go out and just... go at it

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.780
And try to create things and put 'em out there

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.140
There's no wrong answer, I think

00:09:58.140 --> 00:09:59.880
There's a different path for everybody

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:04.120
Sounds like you're kinda taking an approach - a blended approach to it

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:06.960
Which I think that, personally I think that's pretty smart

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:10.240
Just going "Well if we do what..."

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:13.000
"We feel is fun and interesting"

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:16.720
Then it'll... by virtue of that be original and...

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.240
And hopefully it'll have a niche

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.860
That nobody else has, and that's what we set out to do and I think...

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:24.900
Anyone that's ever making anything should always approach it that way, y'know

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:26.660
You have your influences

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:28.800
But at the end of the day you have, ah

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:31.480
What you make and as long as you just try to stay true to that

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:32.800
It comes through

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:33.520
That's pretty good

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:35.440
Sweet man. Well, thank you for coming down

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:36.160
Yup, thank you Jake

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.540
Really appreciate your time and I appreciate you coming down here

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:43.060
And representing everyone out there who's looking to start a career as an artist

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:44.240
I'm gonna get a lot of hate mail

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:45.140
*Jake Wheeze Laugh*

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:45.640
 

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:50.660
The idea that television is an expendable form I believe needs to be eradicated.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:53.900
Because the anatomy of the televisual medium

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:57.440
Is endowed with methods of story telling unobtainable in film

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:01.500
For example, the clearest benefit of a serialized story

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:02.400
